# zlib 1.3.2 in full, hosted: zlib's test/minigzip.c compresses a generated 2 MiB input to the native gzip stream byte for byte and decompresses the native stream.
# minigzip.c (unmodified, from the corpus cache) with example.test's libz.a
# and flags. minigzip-data.py writes the input into %t (2 MiB of text,
# random bytes, a four-letter alphabet, runs and matches up to 32 KiB back,
# and counters, generated the same way on every host; no vendored data) and
# its sha256 is pinned below. At -O0 and at -O2 (plan 5.12.9's levels,
# whatever the leg's), the MSIL program is built (%msil_prog_build),
# verified (%msil_verify) and force-JITed (%msil_jitall), and then:
#   - 'minigzip FILE' compresses a copy of the input into FILE.gz on the
#     leg's runtime (%msil_exec), and the hosted native build of the same
#     sources (%{msil_oracle_flags}: E7h on Linux triples, the DLL C runtime
#     on Windows triples) does the same; the two gzip streams must be equal
#     byte for byte (the header's OS byte comes from the triple, so it is the
#     same; zlib writes no time stamp through gzopen);
#   - 'minigzip -d FILE.gz' of the native stream (of the MSIL stream without
#     a native oracle) must restore the input byte for byte;
#   - on the .NET Framework (msil-run-netfx) the same image compresses the
#     input to the same stream and decompresses the native stream;
#   - so does it on each runtime of the leg's msil_also_run (msil-also-run;
#     for example .NET 10 with DOTNET_TieredCompilation=0, 10+tc0, in the
#     programs and programs-hosted legs), after jitall on that runtime:
#     lit.local.cfg's %{minigzip_also_run_<L>} runs the image with each
#     entry's runtimeconfig.json and environment (lit.cfg.py's
#     config.msil_also_run_cmds, which %{msil_also_run_check} is built
#     from too), and minigzip-data.py's --also
#     (%{minigzip_also_tags}) gives each run its directory and argument file.
# Each run must print nothing and exit 0 (QUIET). minigzip writes its output
# next to its input and removes the input, so each run has a directory of
# its own and gets its file as an absolute path (the programs run from a
# private directory that msil-run.py removes): minigzip-data.py writes each
# path into an argument file that %{readfile:...} reads, in the form the
# runner needs (/mnt/<drive>/... under msil-wsl, where msil-run.py runs the
# MSIL programs and the E7h oracle inside WSL and passes their arguments
# unchanged). The streams never pass through a transcript: minigzip-data.py
# compares the files. That is why the test does not append
# %{msil_also_run_check}, which runs an image with no arguments (minigzip
# would write a gzip stream to stdout) and checks the transcript.
# Plan 5.17.15 (M26).
# REQUIRES: msil-corpus-zlib
# REDEFINE: %{msil_srcs} = %{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/test/minigzip.c
# REDEFINE: %{msil_archives} = z=%{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/adler32.c, \
# REDEFINE:   %{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/compress.c,%{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/crc32.c, \
# REDEFINE:   %{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/deflate.c,%{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/gzclose.c, \
# REDEFINE:   %{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/gzlib.c,%{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/gzread.c, \
# REDEFINE:   %{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/gzwrite.c,%{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/infback.c, \
# REDEFINE:   %{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/inffast.c,%{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/inflate.c, \
# REDEFINE:   %{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/inftrees.c,%{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/trees.c, \
# REDEFINE:   %{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/uncompr.c,%{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2/zutil.c
# REDEFINE: %{msil_copts} = -I%{msil_corpus}/zlib-1.3.2 \
# REDEFINE:   %if msil-triple-linux %{-DZ_HAVE_UNISTD_H -D_LARGEFILE64_SOURCE=1%} \
# REDEFINE:   %else %{-D_CRT_SECURE_NO_DEPRECATE -D_CRT_NONSTDC_NO_DEPRECATE%}

# RUN: rm -rf %t %t.O0.d %t.O0.native.d %t.O2.d %t.O2.native.d
# RUN: %python %S/minigzip-data.py setup %if msil-wsl %{--wsl%} --levels O0,O2 --also=%{minigzip_also_tags} %t | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=INPUT
# INPUT: {{^}}input: 2097152 bytes, sha256 5b714b3fd4a352402c675490e265fb3be2f289118a34d3a1596a9ce27b2e725e{{$}}

# -O0.
